# The reverse proxy for `docker compose --profile proxy up -d`.
#
# Three lines, and two of them are not about TLS:
#
#   - `reverse_proxy web:3000` names the compose service, not a host and a port.
#     Caddy resolves it on the compose network, so nothing here depends on the
#     host's port mapping and the application does not have to be published at
#     all for the proxy to reach it.
#
#   - `client_max_body_size` has no Caddy spelling. It does not need one: Caddy
#     streams request bodies and does not impose a limit of its own, so the 1 MiB
#     nginx default that produces a bare 413 on every upload is a problem this
#     configuration does not have. The application's own `MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE`
#     (20 MiB) is the only cap in the path.
#
#   - HSTS is sent here rather than by the application. `next.config.ts` sends
#     the rest of the security headers and deliberately not this one: a
#     `Strict-Transport-Security` header from a host that is not always served
#     over HTTPS locks browsers out of the plain-HTTP deployment.
#
# `DOMAIN` comes from the environment (compose passes it through). Its default is
# `localhost`, which Caddy answers with a certificate from its internal CA —
# fine for a smoke test on a laptop, and *not* a public certificate. Set
# `DOMAIN=blog.example.com` in `.env` for anything a reader will visit, and set
# `SITE_URL` to the same `https://` origin.

{$DOMAIN:localhost} {
	encode zstd gzip

	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"

	# The proxy adds X-Forwarded-For itself and passes Host through. It must not
	# set a Content-Security-Policy or any of the other headers the application
	# sends: `next.config.ts` scopes those per path, and `/api/assets` carries a
	# narrower one because it serves uploaded SVG.
	reverse_proxy web:3000

	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}
}
